BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Exhibit: The Poetic Landscape
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260514T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260514T180000
UID:2603189lNO4rXKgEqHZ60JMzD7sQ
DESCRIPTION:Date: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE  3/13/26\n\n**APRIL EXHIBIT: THE 
 POETIC LANDSCAPE**\n\nEXHIBIT DATES:  March 30  – April 26\, 2026\n\n*
 *RECEPTION: SATURDAY\, APRIL 4\, 2026\, 3-5 pm: Join us to meet KAREN MILL
 ER and PHYLLIS RAPP\;  celebrate the show and enjoy a poetry reading by f
 eatured guest\, Linda Loveland Reid\, gallery artist and published author.
   Be sure to take part in the Gallery’s free monthly drawing for a beau
 tiful work by a Gallery artist.  Refreshments will be served.**\n\nSonoma
  County artist\, **Karen Miller\,** presents **The Poetic Landscape\,** pa
 stel paintings that convey Miller’s personal experience in connecting wi
 th the intrinsic beauty of natural places. In these pieces\, color\, fluid
  shapes and rhythmic design combine to create an uplifting mood – a true
  poetic interlude.  Evoking a happy mood\, “Geyserville Sunflowers\,”
  turning to the sun by nature\, joyfully dance in its luminescence. By the
  river’s edge\, concentric reflections conjure a calm sense of wellbeing
  in “Fetch.”  The viewer feels welcomed by a warm embrace entering a 
 shaded secret spot in “September Trellis.”  Focused on local scenes\,
  these works truly hone in on the stunning landscape out our doors.  Mill
 er remarked “ As I contemplate the very real places of beauty surroundin
 g us\, I try to express my painterly response in hopes it will engender re
 cognition by the viewers.” \n\nMeet the artist: Miller will be hosting 
 the Gallery on March 30\, April 5 & 15 from 11am to 2:30 pm.\n\n The Stai
 rway Smallworks Showcase features **Phyllis Rapp’s Magic Moments\,** a c
 ollection of watercolors that create an atmosphere that is both introspect
 ive and magical.  Familiar domestic scenes are transformed as humans inte
 ract with animals and birds in floral settings.  In “Silent Watcher” 
 the red-headed young woman’s green eyes lock onto ours as she stares ahe
 ad\, flanked by her avian companion.  Experiencing these works is a like 
 taking a mini-step on the wild side\, an exciting and fun experience.\n\nM
 eet the artist: Rapp will be hosting the Gallery on March 30 and April 10 
 from 11am to 6 pm.\n\nAlso join us for **ART NITE HEALDSBURG**\, Thursday\
 , April 2\, 5 – 6 pm.  The theme for the 2026 kickoff event is Bouquets
  to Art.  ART NITE Healdsburg\, held the 1st Thursday of Every Month\, is
  a community event celebrating art and creativity with over 20 galleries p
 articipating.  ArtNiteHealdsburg.com.\n\nUpstairs Art Gallery\, 306 Cente
 r Street\, Healdsburg\, CA 95448. (707) 431-4214.: OPEN DAILY: 11am – 6 
 pm.    **www.upstairsart gallery.net** \n\nFor further information: Con
 tact: Beverly Bird\, Press Contact\, at bvdrio@sonic.net or 707-799-1490.
LOCATION:Upstairs Art Gallery
URL:http://www.upstairsartgallery.net
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Evening of Contemporary Poetry
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260514T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260514T210000
UID:260314e7TiLbwhDESttZdS8JGYYA
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center's Valley Arts and Lec
 ture Series proudly presents an extraordinary evening of contemporary Am
 erican Poetry featuring two widely published poets living in Woodacre—Aj
 a Couchois Duncan and Albert Flynn DeSilver. Both poets will be celebrat
 ing the release of their new books: Aja's The Intimacy Trials just out f
 rom the University of Chicago Press's Phoenix Poetry Series\, and Albert's
  SOME VOID: New & Selected Poems 1996-2026\, from the nonexistent press w
 hich celebrates work from the past 30 years.\n\nAja Couchois Duncan is a 
 social justice coach and capacity builder of Anishinaabe\, French and Scot
 tish descent living on the ancestral and stolen lands of the Coast Miwok /
  Tamal-ko people. Her debut collection\, Restless Continent \\(Litmus Pres
 s\, 2016\\) was selected by Entropy Magazine as one of the best poetry col
 lections of 2016 and awarded the California Book Award for Poetry in 2017.
  Her latest book is The Intimacy Trials just out from The University of Ch
 icago Press.  \n\n Albert Flynn DeSilver is an internationally publish
 ed poet and writer\, and 30 year resident of Woodacre. Albert taught as a 
 California Poet in the School for many years\, and from 2008-2010 served a
 s Marin County's very first poet laureate. He is the author of numerous bo
 oks of poetry including Letters to Early Street\, the memoir Beamish Boy\,
  a nonfiction book on writing titled Writing as a Path to Awakening from S
 ounds True\, and is currently working on numerous fiction projects. Albert
  has taught at the Esalen Institute\, Omega Institute\, Spirit Rock\, and 
 at writers conferences nationally. He is the development coordinator at th
 e Community Center and hosts the radio and podcast program Voices of the V
 alley on KWMR.
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://events.humanitix.com/evening-of-contemporary-american-poetry
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Barbies & Bags Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260514T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260514T173000
UID:260207xI9iQ5X0OEuuXbxjQJ7nFw
DESCRIPTION:Schedule\n\nSunday\, February 15\, 2026 1:00 PM – 4:00 PM\n\
 nLocation\n\nRoom 201\, Fiber Arts Studio\n\nExperience Level\n\nAll Level
 s\n\nKids\, Programming\, Sustainability\n\nAge\\(s\\)\n\nAll Ages are wel
 come to attend Children under 16 must be accompanied by an adult\n\n$25 
 – One Bag or Doll Entry $40 – Two Bags or Dolls entryTransform plastic
  dolls and create upcycled bags from trash into unique art. Join our hands
 -on Trashion workshop for the Gallery 212 Exhibit and Auction.\n\nAbout th
 e Class\n\nThis hands-on workshop invites participants to rescue and reinv
 ent plastic dolls \\(Barbies\, Kens\, and dolls that are under 12 inches\\
 ) often destined for landfills\, where they can take 20 to 500 years to de
 compose. As part of Trashion Season\, join us for the beloved Gallery 212 
 Exhibit and Auction \\(April 3 – 25\\) where discarded materials are tra
 nsformed into small-scale works of art for a good cause. Together\, we’l
 l reclaim these forgotten figures and reimagine them as expressive\, one-o
 f-a-kind artworks for submission to the gallery exhibit and auction. New t
 his year: Bags! In addition to dolls\, we’re expanding the exhibit to in
 clude bags—think totes\, clutches\, cross bagsm fanny packs\, coin purse
 s\, and backpacks. Just like the Trashion runway\, the only rule is that y
 our piece must be made from unconventional materials and/or upcycled/used 
 textiles. Participants will have access to access to sewing machines\, hot
  glue guns\, irons\, hair dryers and other creative tools as well of a wid
 e selection of trash and upcycled materials to bring their vision to life.
  BYOT \\(Bring your own trash\\) is also welcome and encouraged! The works
 hop registration is your application fee to the gallery exhibit! Come play
 \, experiment\, and collaborate while contributing to a dynamic gallery ex
 hibit that raises funds for Sonoma Community Center programming through th
 e auction. It’s a joy to see your art find a new home—and to be part o
 f a creative community turning trash into treasure.\n\nThank you to Recolo
 gy Sonoma-Marin for donating Barbies & Dolls for our gallery exhibit every
  year.
LOCATION:Sonoma Community Center
URL:https://sonomacommunitycenter.org/programs/thursday-after-school/2026-
 01-08/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
